# Where does the threshold come from - willingness to pay, or what we displace?
next: {"title":"Which uncertainties can more money actually fix?","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/health-technology-assessment-deciding/which-uncertainties-can-more-money.cast"}

## The Line You Have Been Drawing
voice: female
level: advanced
use: cost_effectiveness_plane
with:
    title: The line you have been drawing
    wtp_threshold: 275000
    x_label: ΔE — QALYs gained
    y_label: ΔC — incremental cost (kr)
    points:
      - {label: Nusinersen (SMA), effect: 2.5, cost: 5000000, emphasize: true}
      - {label: Hip replacement, effect: 1, cost: 100000}

You have been drawing a threshold line through this plane for fifteen lectures — this series asks where its slope actually came from, because that one number decides who gets treated.
    draw title x_axis y_axis

Take nusinersen for spinal muscular atrophy: on the order of two to three QALYs per patient, at a list price of seven hundred and fifty thousand dollars in the first year and three hundred and seventy-five thousand every year after.
    draw pt_0 pt_label_0

    pause 0.3

And here, almost hugging the origin, a hip replacement — roughly one QALY for about a hundred thousand kroner.
    draw pt_1 pt_label_1

Same plane, same currency, two orders of magnitude apart in what a krone buys.
    point at.ref pt_1 gesture circle

    pause 0.4

The line between them has slope lambda — around two hundred and seventy-five thousand kroner per QALY, the alternative-cost figure the Magnussen group proposed in 2015, which severity can lift to roughly three times that.
    draw wtp_line wtp_label

Below the line you buy, above it you don't, and nusinersen sits far above — which is why Beslutningsforum said no in 2017, then yes in 2018 with an age limit.
    highlight pt_0 effect glow

    pause 0.5

Now watch a steeper slope: the same drug, the same evidence, quietly becomes cost-effective.
    animate wtp_threshold 2200000 duration 3

Let it fall back and those same children are unaffordable again — nothing about the medicine moved, only a number no trial ever measured.
    animate wtp_threshold 275000 delivery grave duration 2.5

Which is why the slope is fought over: NICE's famous twenty to thirty thousand pounds per QALY was never derived from data, while Claxton and colleagues, estimating what the NHS actually buys at the margin, arrived at about thirteen thousand.
    focus wtp_line wtp_label

    pause 0.5

So four answers are owed to you, one for each part still to come.
    clear

Either lambda is demand-side — what society is willing to pay for a year of health — or supply-side: what the marginal krone already buys somewhere else in the budget.
    text head "Where does λ come from?" x 500 y 630 font_size 34
    text r1 "Demand side — what society will pay" #b5482e x 500 y 530 font_size 26
    text r2 "Supply side — what the marginal krone buys" #2f6b8f x 500 y 455 font_size 26

Then the collision, and what a system should do when its own line is twice as generous as its own productivity.
    text r3 "£20–30,000 against £13,000" x 500 y 380 font_size 26

Then the WHO's rule of one to three times GDP per capita — a threshold its own authors publicly asked the world to stop using.
    text r4 "The WHO rule its authors withdrew" x 500 y 305 font_size 26

And the patients on the far side of the line, who lose health whenever we overpay, and whom no registry ever names.
    text r5 "Who dies when λ is set too high" x 500 y 230 font_size 26

So lambda was never a statement about money we are willing to spend — it is a claim about the health we are about to destroy.
    highlight head effect glow

## Two Ways to Justify Lambda
voice: female
level: advanced

    text title "Two ways to justify λ" x 500 y 690 font_size 32 hidden true
    box n_lambda "Threshold λ" #b5482e x 500 y 590 width 240 hidden true
    box n_demand "Demand side" #8a5fa8 x 270 y 440 width 240 hidden true
    box n_dval "> 1 million kr per QALY" #8a5fa8 x 270 y 290 width 300 hidden true
    box n_supply "Supply side" #2f6b8f x 730 y 440 width 240 hidden true
    box n_sval "≈ 275 000 kr per QALY" #2f6b8f x 730 y 290 width 300 hidden true
    edge edge_0 n_lambda -> n_demand hidden true
    edge edge_1 n_demand -> n_dval hidden true
    edge edge_2 n_lambda -> n_supply hidden true
    edge edge_3 n_supply -> n_sval hidden true

Now that we have seen the threshold drawn as a line through the origin, the honest question is where its slope, lambda, actually comes from — because two respectable ways of answering give numbers four times apart.
    draw title n_lambda

One answer looks outward, at society: how much are we willing to pay for a quality-adjusted life year?
    draw edge_0 n_demand

Norwegian cost-benefit guidance values a statistical life at thirty million kroner — spread over the life years behind that figure, more than a million kroner per life year.
    draw edge_1 n_dval

    pause 0.4

The other answer looks inward, at the budget we already have: what does the last krone in it currently buy?
    draw edge_2 n_supply

Norway's 275,000 kroner per good life year was defended as an estimate of exactly that — the health quietly produced at the margin.
    draw edge_3 n_sval

The left branch is a statement about values — what a life year ought to be worth to us.
    focus n_demand n_dval edge_0 edge_1

The right branch is a measurement — how much health the system loses when that money goes somewhere else.
    focus n_supply n_sval edge_2 edge_3

Two different questions, so no reason their answers should agree — and the gap between them is not academic.
    clear

Say we approve a treatment priced at a million kroner per QALY, costing the system ten million.
    text t_head "Say yes at 1 000 000 kr per QALY" x 500 y 665 font_size 30
    text t_spend "10 million kroner, out of a fixed budget" #8f887c x 500 y 615 font_size 24

Ten million at a million per QALY buys ten quality-adjusted life years, for patients with names and faces.
    path bar_gain closed points [[180,520],[280,520],[280,560],[180,560]] style.fill #87a878
    label lbl_gain "10 QALYs gained" right #87a878 attach_to bar_gain

    pause 0.3

But that money was already busy: at 275,000 kroner per QALY, the services it leaves behind were producing thirty-six.
    path bar_lost closed points [[180,430],[540,430],[540,470],[180,470]] style.fill #c96567
    label lbl_lost "36 QALYs displaced" right #c96567 attach_to bar_lost

So the yes is not a gain of ten — it is a net loss of twenty-six life years.
    text t_net "Net: 26 QALYs destroyed" #b5482e x 500 y 335 font_size 32
    mark mark_net target t_net kind box
    delivery grave

That is the supply-side case, made by Claxton, Culyer and colleagues: with a fixed budget, any lambda above the margin makes the health service destroy health.
    highlight bar_lost effect glow

The demand-side reply stings: a supply-side threshold can only ratify whatever budget politicians happened to set, and can never argue that the budget itself is too small.
    point at.ref t_head gesture underline

Nusinersen shows how the argument actually ends — Beslutningsforum said no in 2017 and yes in 2018, with an age limit and a negotiated price, while the threshold itself never moved.
    text t_close "Nusinersen: no in 2017, yes in 2018 — age limit, negotiated price" #8f887c x 500 y 235 font_size 22

Demand-side lambda asks what a life year is worth to us; supply-side lambda asks what it costs the patients we displace — and only the second number can tell you when a yes does harm.

## 20–30,000 vs 13,000
voice: female
level: advanced
domain: {"x":[0,1],"y":[0,30000]}
sources:
    - id: claxton2015
      title: Methods for the estimation of the National Institute for Health and Care Excellence cost-effectiveness threshold
      authors: Claxton K, Martin S, Soares M, Rice N, Spackman E, Hinde S, Devlin N, Smith PC, Sculpher M
      year: 2015
      finding: The NHS's marginal productivity was estimated at about £12 936 per QALY — below NICE's £20 000–30 000 range.
      doi: 10.3310/hta19140

    axes ax x_label "ΔE — QALYs gained per patient" y_label "ΔC — extra cost (£)" hidden true
    curve nice_high #2f6b8f expr 30000*x hidden true
    curve nice_low #2f6b8f expr 20000*x hidden true
    region nice_band between ["nice_low","nice_high"] style.fill #2f6b8f style.opacity 0.12 hidden true
    label label_nice "NICE: £20–30 000" above-left #2f6b8f attach_to nice_high hidden true

Now that we have seen the two ways to justify lambda — what society says it will pay, and what the budget's last pound already buys — let's put real numbers on both.
    draw ax

This blue band is England's stated number: NICE has judged technologies against twenty to thirty thousand pounds per QALY since 2004.
    draw nice_low nice_high nice_band label_nice

    pause 0.3

And this red line is the other kind of number: in 2015 Karl Claxton and colleagues at York estimated what NHS spending actually produces at the margin — about thirteen thousand pounds per QALY.
    curve claxton_line #b5482e expr 13000*x
    label label_claxton "Claxton 2015: ≈ £13 000" below-right #b5482e attach_to claxton_line cites ["claxton2015"]

Between them sits a wedge of technologies England approves at a price its own health service cannot produce health for.
    region loss_wedge between ["claxton_line","nice_low"] style.fill #c96567 style.opacity 0.18

    pause 0.5

Take a concrete case: a drug gaining half a QALY per patient at ten thousand pounds more than current care.
    dot drug at.x 0.5 at.y 10000 guides true
    label label_drug "New drug: ΔE = 0.5, ΔC = £10 000" above-left attach_to drug

Its ICER — ten thousand divided by nought point five — is twenty thousand pounds per QALY, exactly on the lower edge of the range, so it passes.
    highlight nice_low effect glow

    pause 0.3

But that ten thousand pounds comes out of a budget producing health at thirteen thousand a QALY, so spending it here removes about nought point seven seven QALYs from patients nobody names.
    dot displaced #b5482e at.x 0.769 at.y 10000
    label label_displaced "0.77 QALYs displaced" right #b5482e attach_to displaced

Half a QALY bought, three quarters lost: net monetary benefit at lambda thirteen thousand is minus three thousand five hundred pounds per patient.
    arrow nmb_arrow #b5482e from.x 0.5 from.y 6500 to.x 0.5 to.y 10000
    label label_nmb "NMB at λ = 13 000: −£3 500" left #b5482e attach_to nmb_arrow

Approved and health-destroying at the same time — the difference is entirely which lambda you believe.
    mark mark_nmb target label_nmb kind box
    delivery grave

    pause 0.5

So what should move when the two disagree? Claxton's answer is blunt: if the service produces at thirteen thousand, that is the threshold, and the stated range is simply too high.
    focus claxton_line label_claxton

NICE has not moved its range, and critics of the estimate argue it leans on strong assumptions — that tightening budgets cut the least productive services first, and that national spending data can be read as causal.
    focus nice_low nice_high nice_band label_nice

Culyer and colleagues offered a third reading: a threshold is something to be searched for, not chosen — and if society truly values a QALY at thirty thousand, the gap argues for a bigger budget, not for ignoring the displacement.
    highlight loss_wedge effect glow

Norway took the supply-side route explicitly: an estimated alternative cost of roughly 275 000 kroner per good year, then scaled upward with alvorlighet — severity.
    label label_norway "Norway: 275 000 kr" below #8f887c attach_to claxton_line

Nusinersen, our running example, sat far above every line on this figure — which is why that fight was about price and severity, never about which threshold was right.
    highlight drug effect pulse

A threshold is not a preference: it is a claim about what the last krone in the budget is already producing — and every approval above it is financed in health that never appears in anyone's data.
    delivery grave

## The Patients You Never Meet, Counted
voice: female
level: advanced
sources:
    - id: claxton2015
      title: Methods for the estimation of the National Institute for Health and Care Excellence cost-effectiveness threshold
      authors: Claxton K, Martin S, Soares M, Rice N, Spackman E, Hinde S, Devlin N, Smith PC, Sculpher M
      year: 2015
      finding: The NHS's marginal productivity was estimated at about £12 936 per QALY — below NICE's £20 000–30 000 range.
      doi: 10.3310/hta19140

    text title "One hundred million kroner, two ways to spend it" x 500 y 700 font_size 30 hidden true
    path divider dashed #8f887c points [[500,440],[500,660]] hidden true
    text left_head "The decision you see" #b5482e x 255 y 630 font_size 26 hidden true
    text l1 "100 million kr for a new drug" x 255 y 575 font_size 22 hidden true
    text l2 "ICER ≈ 1,000,000 kr / QALY" x 255 y 530 font_size 22 hidden true
    text l3 "= 100 QALYs gained" #b5482e x 255 y 460 font_size 28 hidden true
    text right_head "The decision you don't" #2f6b8f x 745 y 630 font_size 26 hidden true
    text r1 "The same 100 million kr" x 745 y 575 font_size 22 hidden true
    text r2 "Margin ≈ 275,000 kr / QALY" x 745 y 530 font_size 22 hidden true
    text r3 "= 364 QALYs forgone" #2f6b8f x 745 y 460 font_size 28 hidden true
    text net_text "Net: −264 QALYs" #c96567 x 500 y 370 font_size 36 hidden true
    mark mark_net target net_text kind box hidden true
    text debate_1 "NICE: £20,000–30,000 per QALY" #8a5fa8 x 500 y 660 font_size 24 hidden true
    text debate_2 "Claxton et al. 2015: NHS margin ≈ £12,936" #8a5fa8 cites ["claxton2015"] x 500 y 610 font_size 24 hidden true

Now that we have seen the WHO's own authors withdraw their GDP rule, let's do the arithmetic they were avoiding: what an inflated lambda costs, counted in health.
    draw title divider

On the left, the decision everyone sees: a hundred million kroner for a new drug at roughly a million kroner per QALY — the order of magnitude published analyses report for nusinersen at list price.
    draw left_head l1 l2

That buys a hundred quality-adjusted life years, in children we can name.
    draw l3

    pause 0.4

On the right, the decision nobody reports: in a fixed budget that money leaves care already running at the margin — Norway's 2016 white paper set the reference value at 275,000 kroner per good life year.
    draw right_head r1 r2

Left where it was, the same hundred million produces about three hundred and sixty-four QALYs — statins started, hips replaced, the boring high-volume work.
    draw r3

So the approval is not a gain of a hundred life years. It is a net loss of two hundred and sixty-four.
    draw net_text mark_net delivery grave

Every lambda above the margin runs this subtraction, whether or not anyone writes it down.
    point at.ref net_text gesture underline

    clear keep ["net_text","mark_net"]

One side of it we can see: a named child, a photograph, a hearing — Beslutningsforum said no in 2017, then yes in 2018 with an age limit.
    text head2 "Who are the 264?" x 500 y 520 font_size 30
    person p_named #b5482e x 140 y 260
    label label_named "One named child" below #b5482e attach_to p_named

The other side we cannot: the displaced health is thin and scattered, a delayed hip here, a statin never started there.
    person c1 #8f887c x 400 y 260
    person c2 #8f887c x 460 y 260
    person c3 #8f887c x 520 y 260
    person c4 #8f887c x 580 y 260
    person c5 #8f887c x 640 y 260
    person c6 #8f887c x 700 y 260
    person c7 #8f887c x 760 y 260
    person c8 #8f887c x 820 y 260
    parallel true

Not one of them will ever appear in a record as the person who lost a life year to this decision.
    label label_crowd "264 QALYs, no names" below #8f887c attach_to c4

Thomas Schelling named this in 1968: the identified life gets a headline, the statistical life gets nothing — though it is just as dead.
    focus c1 c2 c3 c4 c5 c6 c7 c8 label_crowd

And the field genuinely splits here: NICE has held twenty to thirty thousand pounds since the early 2000s, a range never derived from evidence about what actually gets displaced.
    draw debate_1

Claxton and colleagues measured the NHS margin at about thirteen thousand; critics call the estimate fragile, extrapolating from mortality to quality of life — the authors answer that even a wide error band leaves NICE above the margin. Unresolved.
    draw debate_2

Which leaves a modest obligation: if you set lambda above the margin, say out loud that you are buying less health, and say what you are buying instead — severity, rarity, a promise kept.
    text closing "Above the margin, you are choosing to buy less health." x 500 y 95 font_size 26
    mark mark_closing target closing kind box

Across this series: the threshold is not what society says it will pay, it is what the next krone already buys — and everything approved above it is paid for by patients you will never meet.
    delivery grave

